WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.370
The brain wants something
new, something fresh.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.550
It's boring to always go
back to the same source.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.480
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and today
I want to talk about the importance

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.850
of variety in language learning.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:15.840
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, uh, click

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.310
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.315 --> 00:00:21.300
If you follow me on a podcast
service, uh, please leave a review.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.930
So today I have a t-shirt on.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.529
Normally, when I go to do these videos,
even though I'm wearing a, t-shirt a

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:34.440
grubby t-shirt for doing tasks around
the house or whatever, I'll put on a nice

00:00:34.445 --> 00:00:40.080
golf shirt or a shirt with a collar for my
video, becasue I feel that that's what I

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.239
should do for all my listeners out there.

00:00:42.519 --> 00:00:46.095
However, in fact that's not necessary.

00:00:46.485 --> 00:00:50.325
And I decided to change things
up to sort of symbolize the

00:00:50.325 --> 00:00:54.764
importance of changing things up
in your language learning routine.

00:00:55.304 --> 00:00:57.375
Variety is extremely important.

00:00:57.585 --> 00:00:59.085
I've mentioned this before.

00:00:59.295 --> 00:01:05.805
I've said the brain requires repetition,
but it also requires novelty.

00:01:06.615 --> 00:01:10.185
And the reason I decided to do this
video is that I was listening to some

00:01:10.185 --> 00:01:15.285
new material in Persian, uh, about the,
uh, Islamic Revolution and the history

00:01:15.285 --> 00:01:18.435
of the Islamic Revolution in a podcast.

00:01:19.335 --> 00:01:24.075
And I started noticing certain words
and phrases that I hadn't noticed before

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.860
when they were in content that I was
used to listening to, and it reminded me

00:01:28.979 --> 00:01:34.020
of how important it is to vary things,
vary the content you're listening to,

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.899
uh, vary the nature of the content.

00:01:36.929 --> 00:01:41.310
In other words, easy content and
more difficult content repeating,

00:01:41.699 --> 00:01:46.229
uh, repetitively listening to things
that, you know, tackling new material.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:47.880
All of this is important.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.285
And, uh, you know, I've mentioned in
a previous video on interleaving that

00:01:54.375 --> 00:02:00.015
that even though people feel that they
can learn something by focusing in on

00:02:00.015 --> 00:02:05.895
it and studying it hard, cramming for
the exam tomorrow, in fact research

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:12.060
uh, on how the brain learns and a
lot of it done by professor Bjork out

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:18.180
of, uh, California, that it's when we
study a bit of this and go off and do

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.320
something else and come back later on
and go back to the first item that we

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:23.880
were studying, that we learn better.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.270
Whenever we block learn, we try to
focus on one thing at a time in fact, we

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.940
are learning less and less every time.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:39.350
Uh, if I focus back or think back on my
learning of Chinese or Japanese, you know,

00:02:39.355 --> 00:02:44.610
I used the sort of repetitive listening in
order to get the rhythm of the language.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:51.270
And so in the case of Chinese, I listened
to these ... dialogues, comic dialogues,

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:56.370
even if I didn't fully understand them
because the, the rhythm, the intonation of

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.650
the language sort of penetrated my brain.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.480
I found that very useful, uh, in
order to improve my pronunciation,

00:03:02.925 --> 00:03:04.095
my feel for the language.

00:03:04.365 --> 00:03:06.975
There was also a, uh, an audio version.

00:03:06.975 --> 00:03:10.965
And of course in those days it was a
great big open reel tape recorder on

00:03:10.965 --> 00:03:13.305
the history of the second world war.

00:03:13.305 --> 00:03:14.755
And I still remember

00:03:15.955 --> 00:03:23.160
...
that was like, he was almost sounded like
he was talking too fast, but I picked

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:30.060
up on the rhythm of that, uh, phrase
"Second World War" and many others.

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:35.640
Uh, similarly, when I was in Japan,
even though I was constantly reading

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.870
new material, eventually reading the
newspapers, reading different books

00:03:40.575 --> 00:03:45.135
with vocabulary lists, glossaries that
I could find in, in the, uh, bookstores

00:03:45.135 --> 00:03:50.295
in Japan, there was one series that I
listened to over and over again, even

00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:51.675
though I didn't fully understand it.

00:03:51.795 --> 00:03:56.329
And that was, I've mentioned this
before, the sort of NHK series

00:03:56.359 --> 00:04:00.299
on ... the history of the Showa Era.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.519
And part of it of course, is that this
audio has to grab you at some level.

00:04:05.519 --> 00:04:10.980
And I enjoyed, enjoyed the, the main
narrator's voice and the excerpts

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.750
from history and I would listen to it.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.560
And it was all part of bringing
that music and that intonation

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.090
of the language into my brain.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:24.340
So that was one form of activity, but
I was also challenging myself with

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.500
new material, different material.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:32.320
So always this sense of variety, um,
variety in terms of your activities,

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:36.160
uh, if you are a person who likes
to do flashcards fine, I'm not a big

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.960
flashcard, as I've explained before.

00:04:38.385 --> 00:04:42.284
But spend some time on vocabulary,
spend some time on grammar.

00:04:42.284 --> 00:04:47.354
Maybe if you're interested, uh, spend some
time listening, spend some time reading.

00:04:47.924 --> 00:04:53.715
The greater variety you introduce into
your learning activity or speaking for

00:04:53.715 --> 00:04:57.075
that matter, obviously the speaking
depends on your opportunity to speak

00:04:57.284 --> 00:05:01.005
more opportunities, if you live where
the language is spoken, but you can

00:05:01.005 --> 00:05:03.645
arrange to speak online with a tutor.

00:05:04.695 --> 00:05:06.284
So variety.

00:05:06.465 --> 00:05:10.185
Have a deliberately, a deliberate
program of introducing variety.

00:05:10.365 --> 00:05:13.635
That's one of the difficulties
in language learning at school.

00:05:13.755 --> 00:05:18.224
Typically there's a textbook and there,
they might even have a, a reader with

00:05:18.224 --> 00:05:23.055
stories in it and you have that textbook
and the reader, and that's all you do.

00:05:23.505 --> 00:05:28.539
That's not very good because after a
while the brain wants something new,

00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:33.460
something fresh, it's boring to always
go back to the same source, the same

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.500
book, the same type of exercises.

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:39.370
So if you are in charge of your
own learning, or even if you

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:43.815
are in a classroom situation,
try to introduce variety.

00:05:44.085 --> 00:05:48.525
It'll help you do better on the things
that you're forced to do in the classroom,

00:05:48.885 --> 00:05:51.585
but it'll because it's gonna make
sure that you improve in the language.

00:05:51.585 --> 00:05:57.975
So I just wanted to, to remind you
of the importance of, of variety,

00:05:57.975 --> 00:05:59.865
even in terms of where you study.

00:05:59.865 --> 00:06:04.455
Sometimes if you can be listening outside
or while jogging or, uh, you know,

00:06:04.455 --> 00:06:06.115
reading in different rooms in the house.

00:06:06.719 --> 00:06:12.270
The extent to which you introduce variety,
it's all beneficial to your learning.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.169
So I did a video on interleaving,
so I would invite you to

00:06:16.169 --> 00:06:17.789
go and have a look at that.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:22.870
And, uh, I think, you know, uh,
one other relevant video, perhaps.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:26.070
So that you can follow up on
this subject, but remember,

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.770
variety is the spice of life.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.840
Variety is gonna help you learn.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.650
And if you're in charge of your
learning, you set the program, you

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:37.740
can change things, you can decide
what you're gonna spend your time on.

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.480
Thank you for listening.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:40.530
Bye for now.

